outputs.exact_statement:
For each fixed pair of leaves there is a continuous real argument $A(\alpha,\beta)$ of $(x_0,x_1)$, periodic under simultaneous translation of $\alpha,\beta$ by $2\pi$. With $A_j=A(G_{b_1}^j(\alpha),G_{b_2}^j(\beta))$, it satisfies $$\label{geo:argument-step}
 -\frac{3\pi}{2}<A_{j+1}-A_j<\frac\pi2,$$ and, for every integer $m$, $$\label{geo:barrier-rule}
 A_j\le a_m:=\frac\pi2+\pi m
       \quad\Longrightarrow\quad A_{j+1}<a_m.$$ If $x_i$ and $x_k$ have opposite strict signs for some $i<k$, then $A_j$ cannot return arbitrarily close to $A_i$ as $j\to+\infty$.

Fix an integer $m\ge2$ and a finite bound $M$, and put $p=m+2$, $r_0=m+15$. There are $R,K,N$, depending only on $m,M$, with the following properties. For $0\le\sigma\le1$, $\|f\|_{r_0,\sigma}\le M$, and $q\ge N$, the action $D_f$ has an interior-stationary mesh with endpoints $X_0=x$, $X_q=x+2\pi$, of the form $$X_j(x)=x+2\pi j/q+w_j(x),\qquad w_0=w_q=0.$$ On the strip of width $\sigma'=(1-K/q^2)\sigma$, $$\label{ana:mesh-bounds}
 q^2\max_j\|w_j\|_{p,\sigma'}+
 q^3\max_j\|w_{j+1}-w_j\|_{p,\sigma'}\le R.$$ The mesh is real symmetric and differentiable in smooth real input variations; its derivative in the norm on the left of <a href="#ana:mesh-bounds" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ana:mesh-bounds">[ana:mesh-bounds]</a> is bounded by $C\|\dot f\|_{r_0,\sigma}$. The corresponding difference bound holds for two inputs. On the real axis the mesh is pointwise unique in the ball with these scaled position and step bounds. Any fixed enlargement of that real uniqueness ball is allowed after increasing $N$.

For fixed $m\ge2$, $M<\infty$, and $r_0=m+15$, there are $K,N$, independent of $0\le\sigma\le1$, such that for $\|f\|_{r_0,\sigma}\le M$ and $|n|\ge N$, $$\label{ana:row-derivative}
 (\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}C_n)_f(v)=[b_n(f)v]_n,$$ where $$\begin{aligned}
 \|b_n(f)-1\|_{m,(1-K/n^2)\sigma}&\le K/|n|,
 \label{ana:row-bound}\\
 \|b_n(f)-b_n(\widehat f)\|_{m,(1-K/n^2)\sigma}
 &\le (K/|n|)\|f-\widehat f\|_{r_0,\sigma}.
 \label{ana:row-difference}
\end{aligned}$$ The second estimate holds for two inputs with the same bound. The derivative identity holds for smooth real variations and for real-symmetric holomorphic variations with the indicated finite norms.

For the actual table, $D=D_u$ is real symmetric and holomorphic in a uniform neighborhood of the real diagonal. It is diagonally $2\pi$-periodic and antisymmetric, and satisfies $$\label{ana:glancing-expansion}
 D(x,x+b)=-\frac{b^3}{24}+O(b^5),\qquad
 D_1(x,x+b)=\frac{b^2}{8}+O(b^4),$$ locally uniformly with each fixed number of derivatives.

There are fixed constants $C_0,B_0$, depending only on the fixed analytic action bounds and candidate smallness bounds, for which the following holds. Suppose $$\|E(V)\|_{\mathcal D_0^1}\le\varepsilon<1,
 \qquad e^{-N\delta}\le\varepsilon,
 \qquad Q=1+N+\delta^{-1},$$ where $N\ge1$ is an integer. If $C_0Q^{B_0}\varepsilon$ is sufficiently small, there is a periodic, real-symmetric, even correction $\Delta V$ such that $$\begin{aligned}
 \max_{0\le j\le3}\|\partial_y^j\Delta V\|_{\mathcal D_{20}^0}
   &\le C_0Q^{B_0}\varepsilon,
   \label{joint:step-size}\\
 \|E(V+\Delta V)\|_{\mathcal D_{20}^1}
   &\le C_0Q^{B_0}\varepsilon^2.
   \label{joint:step-error}
\end{aligned}$$ In particular $B_0$ is independent of the radius $d$, the retained rational parameters, and the order of a formal initializer.

Let $t_0=2\pi l/q\ne0$ be reduced, with $q\le N$ and $|t_0|<d-2\delta$. Under the hypotheses and polynomial smallness conditions of Proposition <a href="#joint:newton" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="joint:newton">15</a>, there are holomorphic periodic jets $w,\dot w$ on $Y_{10}$, real-symmetric in $y$, satisfying $$\begin{aligned}
 w,\dot w&=O_{\mathrm p}(\varepsilon),\notag\\
 E+\partial(a\partial w)&=O_{\mathrm p}(\varepsilon^2),
 \label{joint:value-compatibility}\\
 \bigl(E+\partial(a\partial w)\bigr)^{\displaystyle\cdot}
   &=O_{\mathrm p}(\varepsilon^2).
 \label{joint:jet-compatibility}
\end{aligned}$$ The dot in the last line uses the assigned jet $\dot w$ and differentiates every occurrence of $t$.

Let $\nu\in\{1/2,1\}$, and suppose a mean-zero periodic function $F$ is holomorphic on $\mathcal D_{j_0}^\nu$, with norm at most $M$. Let the fixed integer buffers satisfy $b_{\mathrm{buf}}\ge j_0+1$ and $a_{\mathrm{buf}}\ge j_0$. Suppose, for $1\le |n|\le N$ and every divisor zero with $0<|t_j|<d-b_{\mathrm{buf}}\delta$, that $$|F_n(t_j)|\le\eta e^{-|n|(s-a_{\mathrm{buf}}\delta)}.$$ For the retained modes define $$\begin{aligned}
 R_n(t)&=\sum_{0<|t_j|<d-b_{\mathrm{buf}}\delta} F_n(t_j)
       \frac{\sin(n(t-t_j)/2)}{n(t-t_j)/2},
       \label{joint:interpolation}\\
 H_n(t)&=\frac{F_n(t)-R_n(t)}{d_n(t)},
       \label{joint:quotient}
\end{aligned}$$ and set $H_0=0$ and $H_n=0$ for $|n|>N$. The quotients are holomorphic for $|t|<d-(b_{\mathrm{buf}}+1)\delta$. For each fixed integer $J>\max(a_{\mathrm{buf}},j_0,b_{\mathrm{buf}}+1)$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \|H\|_{\mathcal D_J^{\nu-1/2}}&\le CQ^B(M+\eta),\notag\\
 \|\partial H-F\|_{\mathcal D_J^\nu}
   &\le CQ^B\bigl(\eta+Me^{-N\delta}\bigr).
 \label{joint:division-bounds}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $C,B$ depend on the fixed buffers, not on $N,d,\delta$. Fixed additional derivatives cost fixed additional powers after fixed domain losses. Real symmetry and evenness are preserved.
